  "image_name": "af7fe2a0747875c452550ab07ac05424.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes three related supramolecular strategies for linking components through reversible host–guest interactions. In all cases, colored circles labeled A and B represent functional units such as polymers, nanoparticles, surfaces, targeting groups, reporters, drugs, or pharmacokinetic modifiers, while small outlined shapes indicate guest motifs and bracket-like symbols or ovals indicate host elements.\n\nThe top scheme shows simple molecular conjugation: two separate molecular components are brought together by a host–guest pair to form a noncovalent A–B conjugate. The middle scheme extends the same idea to nanoparticle decorating. A central nanoparticle carrying multiple host sites binds several B-bearing guest molecules, producing a decorated particle with multiple attachments arranged around its surface. This emphasizes multivalent assembly rather than a one-to-one linkage.\n\nThe bottom scheme illustrates “molecular handcuffing,” where two molecular pieces each carry a guest group and are captured together by a bivalent host that encircles both, effectively fastening A and B side by side. Across the figure, the main message is that modular, reversible supramolecular recognition can be used to connect single molecules, functionalize nanoparticles, and clamp together molecular partners in a controlled way.",
